<!-- Speed -->
<section class="sec" id="speed">
  <div class="leg">
    <div class="lane" style="--dx:12px"><svg class="sym wp"><use href="#s-agent"/></svg><p class="lbl"><span class="who">Speed</span></p></div>
    <div class="txt">
      <h2>Move at agent speed, together.</h2>
      <p>An agent can finish a task in minutes. What slows a team down is everything around the code: what's next, who has it, and whether it's really done. Tiller keeps all of that straight, so the agents keep moving and nobody spends the morning chasing status.</p>
      <p class="how-h">How Tiller does it</p>
      <ul class="how">
        <li><a href={url("concepts/#the-tree")}>Plan just far enough ahead</a><p>The work grows from epics to features to tasks. Each feature gets its spec when its turn comes, so nobody plans what will change anyway.</p></li>
        <li><a href={url("skills/")}>Always a next move</a><p><code>tiller next</code> ranks every legal move for your checkout, the decisions waiting on you included.</p></li>
        <li><a href={url("concepts/#contributors-and-claims")}>Work in parallel without collisions</a><p>Claim a task and it's yours. Each one runs on its own branch, so people and agents can work side by side.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <figure class="inset art">
      <span class="inset-t">From the terminal</span>
<pre><i># your ranked next moves, top five</i>
$ <b>tiller next</b>

<i># the same list, then the skill for the top agent move</i>
$ <b>tiller guide</b>

<i># take the next task and go</i>
$ <b>tiller claim task</b> event-night/door-check-in/attendee-search
$ <b>tiller start task</b> event-night/door-check-in/attendee-search</pre>
      <figcaption>Your agent runs these as it works. You can too.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Quality -->
<section class="sec" id="quality">
  <div class="leg">
    <div class="lane" style="--dx:40px"><svg class="sym wp"><use href="#s-both"/></svg><p class="lbl"><span class="who">Quality</span></p></div>
    <div class="txt">
      <h2>Done means done, every time.</h2>
      <p>Speed only helps if the work holds up. With Tiller, every piece of work knows what done means before anyone starts it, and it can't get there without passing the checks you chose.</p>
      <p class="how-h">How Tiller does it</p>
      <ul class="how">
        <li><a href={url("concepts/#the-lifecycle-and-its-gates")}>Acceptance criteria up front</a><p>A feature's spec, its acceptance criteria, and its tasks are written and approved before the first line of code.</p></li>
        <li><a href={url("concepts/#gates-and-evidence")}>Checks that block done</a><p>Build, tests, and evals run on every task, and the result is recorded with the revision it ran against.</p></li>
        <li><a href={url("concepts/#review-grain")}>Review at the grain that fits</a><p>A pull request per task, per feature, or per epic. There's a code review skill when you want a second set of eyes.</p></li>
        <li><a href={url("concepts/#corrections-drift-and-amendments")}>Drift gets caught</a><p>Once a spec is approved, it's pinned. If it moves after that, Tiller flags it before work goes on.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <figure class="inset art">
      <span class="inset-t">Gates, declared in tiller.yml</span>
<pre>gates:
  required:
    evals: tiller eval …              on: [feature]
    build: bin/rails zeitwerk:check   on: [feature, task]
    tests: bin/rails test             on: [feature, task]
  optional:
    code_review: attestation
    security_review: attestation</pre>
      <figcaption>Your project's own commands. Tiller runs them and writes down what happened.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Confidence -->
<section class="sec" id="confidence">
  <div class="leg">
    <div class="lane" style="--dx:16px"><svg class="sym wp"><use href="#s-you"/></svg><p class="lbl you"><span class="who">Confidence</span></p></div>
    <div class="txt">
      <h2>Know where things stand, and know it's right.</h2>
      <p>You shouldn't need a status meeting to know how the project is going, or a leap of faith to merge. Tiller gives everyone the same picture and keeps a person's hand on every merge.</p>
      <p class="how-h">How Tiller does it</p>
      <ul class="how">
        <li><a href={url("concepts/#who-does-what")}>A person merges, every time</a><p>An agent can carry the work right up to the line and turn every check green. Only a human merge gets it to done, and Tiller sees it by watching git.</p></li>
        <li><a href={url("console/#build")}>A board everyone can read</a><p>Every feature from draft to done, who's on it, and how many of its tasks are finished. Home leads with the decisions waiting on you.</p></li>
        <li><a href={url("console/#explore")}>Every detail one click deep</a><p>Explore walks the plan from epic to task, and every task carries its spec, its checks, and its history.</p></li>
        <li><a href={url("console/#report")}>A report you can hand a client</a><p>Burn-up, throughput, bugs, lead and cycle time, and epic progress, with no people on it.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="art shots">
      <figure class="inset">
        <span class="inset-t">Build, on a sample project</span>
        <img class="only-light" src={url("console-build-light.webp")} alt="The Build board: features in six columns from Draft to Done, each card showing its epic, contributors, and tasks finished" width="1440" height="542">
